Q: How to use images when it comes to SQL databases? A: <p>In my experience, it is better to store images and other files into the file systems and the meta data in a database table. I, for example, use a directory structure like DATADIR/xxx/yyyyyxxx.bin where yyyyyxxx is the id with leading zeros and xxx the id modulo 1000. The exact breakdown is based on the number of expected files and can also consist of three levels (e.g., zzzzyyxx).</p>
<p>In this example, I have 1000 file directories that fill up evenly. The meta table contains all the important information such as filename, MIME type, and size. Delivery to the website is handled by an agent that calculates the source from the ID and delivers the file with its original name.</p>
<p>PHP example to get the storage filename:</p>
<pre><code>function GetStorageFilename ( $id )
{
    return sprintf(&quot;%s/%03u/%08u.bin&quot;, STORAGE_DIR, $id % 1000, $id );
}
</code></pre>
<p>PHP example code for delivering a file. <code>$info</code> is is a record from the database:</p>
<pre><code>header('Content-Type: '.$info-&gt;mimetype);
header(&quot;Content-Disposition: attachment; filename=\&quot;{$info-&gt;filename}\&quot;&quot;);
readfile(GetStorageFilename($info-&gt;id));
</code></pre>
<p>Btw, <code>$info-&gt;filename</code> is normalized: Directory names are removed, special characters are removed or replaced, and the file name length is limited.</p>
<p>With this concept it is possible to store 2 or versions of a file with same filename.</p>

Q: yii how to show the custom error msg with validation failed msg A: <p>If you use the custom validation function, then:</p>

<pre><code> class Post extends CActiveRecord
 {
     public function rules()
     {
         return array(
             array('expire_date_error', 'check'),
         );
     }

     public function check()
     {
         if($this-&gt;a &gt; $this-&gt;b) {
             $this-&gt;addError('expire_date_error', 'New custom error message');
             return false;
         }

         return true;
     }
 }
</code></pre>

